# The instruction a `step` into a function stops on (design.md §3.1, as
# panel 200's R4 amended it for defect 472; defect 2038): one `nop` on the
# function's own line, at the end of the prologue's one physical line, before
# the jump to the first block, and the lines each unit's preamble defines the
# word by.
#
# WHY. A debugger's `step` into a function stops at the first instruction
# after the frame's setup, the one the line table flags `prologue_end`, and
# design.md §3.1 promises that this is on the function's `function` line. The
# prologue's line ends `goto bb0;`, and `bb0` follows it at once. clang for
# arm64 keeps that jump, a `b` on the function's line flagged
# `prologue_end`; clang for x86-64 drops a jump to the next instruction, so
# where nothing else on the line makes code, no zeroed slot and no thread
# guard, its `prologue_end` is the first statement's, one line on, and every
# later step is a line on too (the CI's Linux x86-64 leg, run 38317587830).
# A `nop` is an instruction on the function's line for both: with the
# `stepping` case's C, x86-64's `prologue_end` moved to its line 6 and
# arm64's stayed there (the coordinator, `xcrun dwarfdump --debug-line` on
# this Mac, 2026-10-13).
#
# ONLY UNOPTIMISED. Where `__OPTIMIZE__` is defined the word is `((void)0)`,
# which makes no code: stepping and variables are the `-O0` build's (panel
# 197), and an optimised function keeps no instruction it does not need.
# `volatile`, so that no pass may take the `nop` away as having no effect.

# The statement, as the prologue's line writes it before the jump.
constant WORD: str
    "HERO_ENTRY_STOP;"

# The lines each unit's preamble defines the word by.
constant DEFINE: [str]
    [
        "#ifdef __OPTIMIZE__"
        "#define HERO_ENTRY_STOP ((void)0)"
        "#else"
        "#define HERO_ENTRY_STOP __asm__ volatile(\"nop\")"
        "#endif"
    ]
